ment accounts, giving crypto and fintech firms a clearer path to core U.S. payment rails.\nURL Slug: fed-master-accounts-crypto-access-plan\nCategories: Latest News, Regulation\nPrimary Keyword: Fed master accounts\nSecondary Keywords: limited payment accounts, Fed payment rails, Ripple stablecoin\n\n# Fed master accounts open 60-day path for crypto firms\n\nThe Federal Reserve has reopened one of the most important policy debates for digital asset firms. According to [CoinDesk](https://www.coindesk.com/policy/2026/05/20/federal-reserve-proposes-limited-master-accounts-long-pursued-by-crypto-firms), the central bank started a 60-day comment period on limited payment accounts that would let eligible firms clear and settle payments through the Fed without getting full master-account status.\n\nThat matters because direct payment access can cut settlement costs and reduce dependence on partner banks. For traders, the immediate read is not a revenue jump, but a higher probability that firms tied to stablecoins, custody, and exchange settlement could gain faster dollar rails over time.\n\nThis is a policy trade, not a headline trade.\n\n## Limited payment accounts: What the Fed master accounts plan signals\n\nThe proposal gives crypto markets something they have lacked for years, a clearer framework instead of one-off fights with regional Reserve Banks. [Decrypt](https://decrypt.co/368431/trump-orders-fed-to-review-crypto-firms-access-to-master-accounts) also reported that the White House ordered the Fed to review whether non-bank financial firms can legally receive direct access to payment accounts within 120 days.\n\nThat combination matters. The Fed is moving through notice-and-comment rulemaking just as the administration pushes the legal question into the open. The result is a more visible policy path, which tends to matter for markets well before any firm actually receives a new account.\n\n### The 60-day window turns a niche concept into a live rulemaking process\n\nThe Fed's revised proposal is close to the framework floated in December, but it is more concrete. Firms would be able to use payment accounts for clearing and settlement, which is the part of Fed access that crypto exchanges, stablecoin issuers, and fintech payment firms care about most.\n\nA formal comment period does not mean approval is guaranteed. It does mean the idea is no longer stuck at the rumor stage. Markets can now model a timetable, identify likely beneficiaries, and track how much resistance comes from banks, lawmakers, and the Reserve Banks themselves.\n\nIf the framework survives with only limited changes, it would mark a shift from discretionary case-by-case handling toward a published standard. That lowers uncertainty, which is often the first thing policy-sensitive assets reprice.\n\n### The product is narrower than a full master account, and that is the point\n\nThe revised account would not offer intraday credit, discount window access, or interest on balances. Automated controls would also be used to prevent overdrafts. That structure tells traders the Fed is trying to separate payment utility from full banking privileges.\n\nThe most interesting tweak is the change to closing-balance limits. After earlier feedback, the Fed said those limits would be tied to expected payment activity and that the maximum closing balance was increased. If that survives the final rule, the account becomes more practical for firms handling large settlement flows, especially stablecoin reserve operations and exchange-related fiat transfers.\n\nFor the market, that makes this less about symbolism and more about throughput. A limited account with balance caps that are too low would be mostly cosmetic. A limited account sized to real payment activity could change how crypto-linked firms manage dollar liquidity.\n\n## Key Fed payment rails signals traders should watch\n\nThis is now a timeline story. The winners are not necessarily the firms with the loudest political support, but the ones whose business models fit a narrow payments account and can satisfy bank-grade oversight.\n\nPolicy-sensitive names such as Ripple, Circle, Coinbase, and Kraken will stay in focus because each has a reason to want faster reserve movement, redemption processing, or institutional dollar settlement. The policy edge is strongest for companies that already sit near the banking perimeter through trust structures, custody operations, or payment-heavy business lines.\n\n### Trump put a 120-day legal review on the clock\n\nThe executive order does not force the Fed to grant access. It does force the central bank to explain, on a deadline, whether current law allows broader payment-account access for uninsured depository institutions and non-bank financial companies.\n\nThat changes the policy setup. A denial would no longer happen quietly inside supervisory channels. It would sit next to a public review ordered from the White House, which raises the odds of a more detailed legal and political fight. For traders, that means more event-driven volatility around comment letters, speeches, and any sign that the Fed board and regional banks are not aligned.\n\nIt also widens the debate beyond crypto exchanges. If the review opens the door for fintech payment firms and stablecoin issuers together, the market will start pricing a broader infrastructure theme instead of a single-company catalyst.\n\n### Ripple stablecoin names are the clearest watchlist\n\nRipple, Coinbase, Circle, and Kraken sit near the front of the line because they have the clearest use cases for direct or limited payment access. Ripple's RLUSD business would benefit from faster reserve transfers and redemption handling, while exchanges and custodians would gain cleaner fiat settlement.\n\nThere is still a timing ceiling. Regional Reserve Banks have been encouraged to pause new Tier 3 applications while a permanent rule is finished, with reporting pointing to December 2026 as the outside